Updates to our Drupal CMS and similar internal platforms rarely look exciting from the outside. But we can make habit of framing the impact in terms leadership understands: transforming “maintenance” to “mission.”

**TL;DR:** The real work isn't just about shipping code, but about consistently telling the story of how that code benefits the organization. Our recent Drupal update is a perfect example of a repeatable strategy to make invisible work visible.

If you’ve ever shipped a Drupal update, you know the drill: new release, a couple of small features, a few bug fixes, maybe some backend speed tweaks. It’s the kind of thing that’s vital, but doesn’t win standing ovations in the staff meeting.

And yet a recent release gave us something far more tangible: an estimated **58 hours a month** reclaimed across our editorial teams. That’s not just a tech stat: it’s 1.5 extra work weeks a month we can now spend on strategy instead of image crops.

That’s the story worth telling. And that’s what this post is really about: a framework for how we can strategically and recurringly make this kind of internal work visible.

## The Paradox of Invisible Work

The paradox of CMS and internal tools work:

* The better they are, the less anyone notices them.
* Their value lives in avoided pain and gained efficiency, not shiny new features.
* Leadership (understandably) sees the line item cost, but not the day-to-day friction removed.

If we want these updates to land as more than “IT costs,” we have to tell the story in human, strategic terms. A faster menu load time is trivia. **Fifty-eight hours gifted back to staff time? That’s strategy.**

This doesn’t happen by accident. It takes a conscious effort to translate the technical work into organizational value.

## A Framework for Making "Maintenance" Visible

Here's a simple, three-part framework we used to talk about our latest Drupal update that applies to any internal work.

### 1. Show the Pain, Quantify the Gain

Before you even touch the code, you need to understand the human cost of the current process. What’s the tedious, repetitive task that's eating up your team’s time? Document the "before" state and the inefficiency.

For us, the pain was obvious. The "Photoshop shuffle" was a real thing: a clunky, nine-step process just to get a cover image for a PDF. We saw the time sink and the user frustration.

We translated that pain into a quantifiable win. We weren't just "fixing a bug" or "adding a feature." We were saving a concrete amount of time per task, which then aggregated into a much bigger number: **58 hours a month.**

### 2. Connect the Win to the Mission

This is the most critical step. Your work isn't an end in itself; it’s a means to an end. After you’ve quantified the gain, you must tie it back to the organization’s core purpose.

Our mission is to create content that serves our audience. Less time spent wrestling with the CMS means more time creating content, better content, and more time to think strategically. The platform work isn’t overhead—it’s a direct investment in the mission.

This is the difference between a project report and a compelling story.

### 3. Share the Story Widely and Simply

Don't just bury this information in a technical memo. Share it with key stakeholders in a format they understand.

Here is an anonymized and condensed version of the update I sent to stakeholders. It’s simple, direct, and leads with the value, not the tech.

> **Subject:** Happy News: latest platform release reclaims 1.5 work weeks per month for editors
>
> Hi Team,
>
> Thanks to the efforts of our development team, our latest release delivered tangible improvements that ease editors’ work, reduce friction, and elevate content quality.
>
> The headline: These changes reclaim an estimated 58 hours monthly. In aggregate, this effectively gifts the organization 1.5 extra work weeks each month to reinvest in strategic initiatives. That’s the power of platform standardization: less friction, more time for mission-critical work.
>
> **Key Example: Document Processing**
>
> ## Before: 9 steps – often inconsistent and error-prone
>
> 1. Create entry for PDF
> 2. Open Photoshop
> 3. Crop PDF cover
> 4. Save file
> 5. Create media entry for PDF cover
> 6. Upload image of PDF cover
> 7. Name entry
> 8. Specify 6 different image crops
> 9. Select image for PDF cover
>
> ## After: 3 steps
>
> 1. Create entry for PDF
> 2. System automatically generates an image of the PDF cover
> 3. Select image, overriding if desired

This simple before-and-after makes the relief tangible, even for non-technical leadership.

