## A small tech appendix (for the Drupal crowd)

For those curious about the “how”: much of this gain came from three custom code aspects in our latest Drupal 10.x release:

- **A lighter admin menu.** We dropped the heavy contrib admin toolbar (too many options editors never needed, [plus it was query-heavy](/posts/20250710-metrics-of-caution-azure-mysql/)). In its place, we built a lean custom menu focused only on what our editors actually use.
- **Auto-generated cover images for PDFs.** A new module now auto-creates a Media image entry when uploading a Media Document (PDF), thanks to ImageMagick. That cut the nine-step “Photoshop shuffle” down to three.
- **Simplifying image cropping.** We’ve dropped use of the [`image_widget_crop`](https://www.drupal.org/project/image_widget_crop) module in favor of [`focal_point`](https://www.drupal.org/project/focal_point) along with some custom UX wins, which handles 99% of what we need while dropping unnecessary complexity.

---

## Why this matters beyond a single win

Yes, this was a specific Drupal release. But the bigger mission here is about how we can **communicate the value and impact of any technical work on any internal platform.** The code itself won’t sell the value. We need a repeatable, consistent way to do it ourselves.

We need leadership to see CMS updates not as overhead, but as capacity multipliers. That starts with emails, updates, and blog posts that frame them as such. By documenting the pain, quantifying the gain, and connecting it back to the mission, we can turn invisible work into an indispensable part of the company's story.

